HHIISSTTOORRYY
       The ddeelleettee__mmoodduullee() system call is not supported by glibc.  No declara‐
       tion is provided in glibc headers, but, through  a  quirk  of  history,
       glibc  versions  before  glibc  2.23  did export an ABI for this system
       call.  Therefore, in order to employ this system call,  it  is  (before
       glibc  2.23) sufficient to manually declare the interface in your code;
       alternatively, you can invoke the system call using ssyyssccaallll(2).

   LLiinnuuxx 22..44 aanndd eeaarrlliieerr
       In Linux 2.4 and earlier, the system call took only one argument:

          iinntt ddeelleettee__mmoodduullee((ccoonnsstt cchhaarr **_n_a_m_e));;

       If _n_a_m_e is NULL, all unused modules marked auto‐clean are removed.

       Some further details of differences in the behavior of  ddeelleettee__mmoodduullee()
       in  Linux  2.4  and  earlier are _n_o_t currently explained in this manual
       page.

NNOOTTEESS
       The uninterruptible sleep that may occur if OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK is omitted  from
       _f_l_a_g_s  is  considered undesirable, because the sleeping process is left
       in an unkillable state.  As at Linux 3.7, specifying OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK is  op‐
       tional, but in future kernels it is likely to become mandatory.

